WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:10.220
Welcome back to 8.701.

00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:14.120
In this lecture, we'll
talk about CP symmetry

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:17.680
or CP violation.

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:19.420
In previous lectures,
we discussed

00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:23.710
that the weak interaction is
not invariant under parity

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:25.930
and charge conjugation
transformation.

00:00:25.930 --> 00:00:29.500
But now we can ask the
question, how about CP--

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:33.550
so transformation which does
change conjugation and parity

00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:35.140
transformation.

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:37.930
The classical example
to show parity violation

00:00:37.930 --> 00:00:40.250
is the decay of a pion.

00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:45.660
So we have here this
charge pion with spin 0.

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:49.270
And it decays into a muon
and a neutrino, an anti-muon

00:00:49.270 --> 00:00:50.590
and a neutrino.

00:00:50.590 --> 00:00:56.180
And so since the neutrino
is left-handed, the--

00:00:56.180 --> 00:00:58.700
[INAUDIBLE] decay
coming onto muon

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:01.500
needs to be left-handed as well.

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:04.190
So if you do parity
transformation of this decay,

00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:08.270
you see that the outcoming
muon would be right-handed.

00:01:08.270 --> 00:01:11.750
On the other hand, there is
no right-handed neutrino.

00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:14.950
And therefore, this
decay is not possible.

00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:16.350
So this is not--

00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:19.590
so this mirror symmetry
is not realized in nature,

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:22.350
as a consequence of
the weak interaction.

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:25.290
So similarly, we could do a
charge transformation, a charge

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:28.070
conjugation, of this decay.

00:01:28.070 --> 00:01:30.930
So you turn particles
into the antiparticles.

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:34.140
And you find here
this antineutrino,

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:35.100
which is left-handed.

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:37.350
And also, those don't
exist in nature.

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:40.590
So parity or charge
conjugation doesn't really

00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:43.230
work on those decays,
those [INAUDIBLE] decays.

00:01:43.230 --> 00:01:46.980
But what does work, if you
apply the parity and a charge

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:52.860
conjugation, we turn the
positively-charged pion

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:57.750
into a negatively-charge pion,
the antimuon into a muon,

00:01:57.750 --> 00:02:00.240
and the neutrino
into an antineutrino.

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:05.440
And you see here that the
antineutrino is right-handed.

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:07.110
So is the muon.

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:10.850
And so that decay is
actually observed in nature.

00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:11.690
Good.

00:02:11.690 --> 00:02:13.640
So we saved the day.

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:18.140
It seems like that CP,
that the weak interaction

00:02:18.140 --> 00:02:21.410
is invariant in the
CP transformation.

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:25.640
However, that's not quite true.

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:32.210
Gell-Mann and Pais noted that
in systems of neutral kaons,

00:02:32.210 --> 00:02:33.480
there's an artifact.

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:36.410
And the fact is that
a particle, a K0,

00:02:36.410 --> 00:02:42.550
can turn into an antiparticle
by changing the strangeness.

00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:47.770
And that's possible in
this kind of box diagrams,

00:02:47.770 --> 00:02:51.460
which include a box
with a couple of W's.

00:02:54.400 --> 00:03:00.340
And it's easy to see that
if you could prepare a kaon,

00:03:00.340 --> 00:03:03.640
it will oscillate, because
those diagrams are possible,

00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:05.920
into an antiparticle.

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:10.150
So now what is happening
now to CP here,

00:03:10.150 --> 00:03:15.080
if I apply CP on a kaon, I find
a minus sign and an antikaon.

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:17.080
So if you want to
analyze this further,

00:03:17.080 --> 00:03:19.760
you might want to find
the eigenstates to this.

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:22.570
And so the eigenstates can
be found, as well as K1

00:03:22.570 --> 00:03:28.860
and K2, which are admixtures
of the K0 and the anti-K0.

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:31.360
And you find this
symmetric, the symmetric

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:33.530
and the anti-symmetric states.

00:03:33.530 --> 00:03:34.460
Good.

00:03:34.460 --> 00:03:37.300
So if you apply CP
on the eigenstates,

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:41.170
you find eigenvalues
of 1 and minus 1.

00:03:41.170 --> 00:03:46.630
It turns out that the
lifetime of decay 1 decay 2,

00:03:46.630 --> 00:03:50.350
those eigenstates,
is very different.

00:03:50.350 --> 00:03:53.080
One is 10 to the minus
10, and one is 5 times 10

00:03:53.080 --> 00:03:54.550
to the minus 8.

00:03:54.550 --> 00:03:58.990
So decay 1 decays much,
much quicker than decay 2.

00:03:58.990 --> 00:04:03.860
So this, then, sets the stage
to a test of CP violation.

00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:08.500
So what you're going to do is
prepare a beam of K0's and let

00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:10.990
them decay.

00:04:10.990 --> 00:04:14.470
And only after some time,
you study the beam again,

00:04:14.470 --> 00:04:18.850
which then should be
made up solely of K2's.

00:04:18.850 --> 00:04:22.330
So if you in that beam
observe decays of the K2

00:04:22.330 --> 00:04:28.270
into two pions, you noted that
there is an admixture again,

00:04:28.270 --> 00:04:30.520
which violates CP.

00:04:30.520 --> 00:04:34.450
So you have an admixture of K1's
in a beam which should just be

00:04:34.450 --> 00:04:36.220
of K2's.

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:41.500
So that mixture, then,
will violate CP invariance.

00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:43.630
And exactly that was done.

00:04:43.630 --> 00:04:46.360
So Croning and Fitch
picked up this idea.

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:50.230
They set up an experiment in
which they produced kaons.

00:04:50.230 --> 00:04:51.520
They had them decay.

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:53.800
And then they studied
later in the beam

00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:57.370
whether or not they could
find two pion decays.

00:04:57.370 --> 00:05:00.264
And they did,
indeed, observe 42--

00:05:00.264 --> 00:05:08.840
45 pion decays, two-pion decays,
in a total of 22,700 decays.

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:13.570
So that means that this K long
beam, the long-lived kaon beam,

00:05:13.570 --> 00:05:18.310
is actually an admixture of
K2's with a small additional

00:05:18.310 --> 00:05:20.710
component of K1's.

00:05:20.710 --> 00:05:26.450
So here they
observed CP violation

00:05:26.450 --> 00:05:30.020
through the mixture
of those states.

00:05:30.020 --> 00:05:32.340
And this epsilon
gives you, you know,

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:37.770
size of the strength
of the CP violation.

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:39.480
So here is a note of the paper.

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:41.790
We'll have another
discussion of this in class

00:05:41.790 --> 00:05:44.970
by a student presentation,
Croning and Fitch.

00:05:44.970 --> 00:05:48.150
Here this is Croning,
and this is Fitch.

00:05:48.150 --> 00:05:53.010
It turns out that Croning
is actually a student,

00:05:53.010 --> 00:05:55.200
or was a student, of
Enrico Fermi and also

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:57.460
worked in Chicago.

00:05:57.460 --> 00:05:59.370
So that's quite an
interesting family tree

00:05:59.370 --> 00:06:03.900
here, to which also
Jerry Friedman belongs.

00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:07.320
Jerry Friedman is a
retired faculty at MIT

00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:10.060
and discovered that protons
are made out of quarks.

00:06:10.060 --> 00:06:12.130
So this is a very
interesting family tree.

00:06:12.130 --> 00:06:14.472
If you have some time, you
might want to look into this.

00:06:14.472 --> 00:06:15.555
But here's the experiment.

00:06:15.555 --> 00:06:18.630
So you take protons, you
dump them into a beam.

00:06:18.630 --> 00:06:24.270
You try to, with this magnet,
filter out a neutral component,

00:06:24.270 --> 00:06:28.800
get rid of all photons, and
then let this beam decay,

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:32.280
and look in the spectrometer
for decays of two photons.

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:34.690
Here's a bigger picture
of the same spectrum.

00:06:34.690 --> 00:06:37.680
So this is actually a
blow-up view of this.

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:41.820
So you have your kaons, neutral
kaons coming in, the K2's.

00:06:41.820 --> 00:06:44.790
And then you look
for pion decays.

00:06:44.790 --> 00:06:47.820
The instrumentation and how
we actually would do this

00:06:47.820 --> 00:06:50.190
is part of later discussions
where we actually talk

00:06:50.190 --> 00:06:54.740
about detectors in more detail.

00:06:54.740 --> 00:06:55.780
All right.

00:06:55.780 --> 00:07:00.310
So we just saw that Croning
and Fitch observed CP violation

00:07:00.310 --> 00:07:02.590
in mixture of states.

00:07:02.590 --> 00:07:06.500
But we can also observe CP
violation in direct decays.

00:07:06.500 --> 00:07:09.505
And the classical example
here is the case of the K

00:07:09.505 --> 00:07:11.730
long and semileptonic decays.

00:07:11.730 --> 00:07:15.250
So semileptonic here means we
have a decay of the K long,

00:07:15.250 --> 00:07:18.670
a neutral particle and a
charged pion, an electron

00:07:18.670 --> 00:07:20.350
and antineutrino--

00:07:20.350 --> 00:07:26.080
or it might very well also decay
into a pi minus, a positron,

00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:27.610
and a neutrino.

00:07:27.610 --> 00:07:31.750
And it turns out, when you
really count those events

00:07:31.750 --> 00:07:34.570
and perform a
precise experiment,

00:07:34.570 --> 00:07:37.660
that the K longs prefer
decays to positrons

00:07:37.660 --> 00:07:39.700
over decays to electrons.

00:07:39.700 --> 00:07:42.850
And so the fractional
amount of this imbalance

00:07:42.850 --> 00:07:45.730
is 3 times 10 to the minus 3.

00:07:45.730 --> 00:07:48.850
So this is a rather small
effect, again, of CP violation

00:07:48.850 --> 00:07:52.420
here in direct decays.

00:07:52.420 --> 00:07:54.370
Since then, CP
violation has also

00:07:54.370 --> 00:07:57.130
been shown in the
decay of B mesons.

00:07:57.130 --> 00:07:59.320
And the program of
studying B mesons

00:07:59.320 --> 00:08:04.510
is a big part of the LHC
experiment at the LHC.

00:08:04.510 --> 00:08:07.520
There's also experiments
in Japan going on right now

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:14.380
which study B mesons in order to
learn further about B systems.

00:08:14.380 --> 00:08:16.360
Tests are also
underway, for those

00:08:16.360 --> 00:08:19.310
who listened to the colloquium
on Monday, in the neutrino

00:08:19.310 --> 00:08:20.170
sector.

00:08:20.170 --> 00:08:22.510
So here we have a
completely different part,

00:08:22.510 --> 00:08:26.350
so not quarks are involved in
weak interaction but neutrinos.

00:08:26.350 --> 00:08:28.060
And so the question
is whether or not

00:08:28.060 --> 00:08:32.159
in that sector of physics, that
sector of the standard model,

00:08:32.159 --> 00:08:34.150
there is CP violation.

00:08:34.150 --> 00:08:36.820
Those are aspects we'll
discuss later on when we talk

00:08:36.820 --> 00:08:40.270
about neutrinos specifically.

00:08:40.270 --> 00:08:46.380
Before I close, a
few more remarks

00:08:46.380 --> 00:08:51.130
on the matter-antimatter
symmetry.

00:08:51.130 --> 00:08:54.540
So one of the biggest mysteries
in physics, I would claim,

00:08:54.540 --> 00:08:58.000
is the fact that we're even
here to ask this question.

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:00.090
So there is
apparently more matter

00:09:00.090 --> 00:09:02.070
in the universe than antimatter.

00:09:02.070 --> 00:09:04.620
You start from a big bang,
there was this symmetry,

00:09:04.620 --> 00:09:08.080
and now we live in a universe
which is dominated by matter.

00:09:08.080 --> 00:09:10.120
So how is this possible?

00:09:10.120 --> 00:09:15.570
So in 1967, Zakharov
proposed that this

00:09:15.570 --> 00:09:20.230
is possible in a system where
baryon number is violated.

00:09:20.230 --> 00:09:21.930
So this is almost a
trivial statement.

00:09:21.930 --> 00:09:23.400
If you start from
an equal number

00:09:23.400 --> 00:09:27.300
of baryons and antibaryons,
the sum is then 0.

00:09:27.300 --> 00:09:29.940
The baryon number
is 0 of this system.

00:09:29.940 --> 00:09:32.880
And you end up in a system
which is dominated by baryons,

00:09:32.880 --> 00:09:36.030
then baryon number
needs to be violated.

00:09:36.030 --> 00:09:39.040
But there's also the need
of CP violation in this.

00:09:39.040 --> 00:09:43.470
So we just saw that this
is realized in nature.

00:09:43.470 --> 00:09:46.560
But the amount of CP violation
we observe in the system I just

00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:49.830
discussed is not
sufficient to explain

00:09:49.830 --> 00:09:52.830
the matter-antimatter
symmetry we observe in nature.

00:09:52.830 --> 00:09:54.150
So there is more to be found.

00:09:54.150 --> 00:09:59.250
There's new physics to be
looked for in CP violation

00:09:59.250 --> 00:10:00.780
on this overall question.

00:10:00.780 --> 00:10:04.740
And there's also a
need for the actions

00:10:04.740 --> 00:10:06.840
to be out of
equilibrium, meaning

00:10:06.840 --> 00:10:10.225
that you don't revert the
processes as you go forward.

00:10:13.660 --> 00:10:16.540
Yet another point of
discussion, which I will not

00:10:16.540 --> 00:10:19.030
go into much detail
in this lecture,

00:10:19.030 --> 00:10:22.870
is that our quantum
field theory, which

00:10:22.870 --> 00:10:25.750
describes quasi-standard
model and describes

00:10:25.750 --> 00:10:29.360
the interaction of
particles, is invariant,

00:10:29.360 --> 00:10:32.290
and the CPT transformations.

00:10:32.290 --> 00:10:35.110
That means that
if CP is violated,

00:10:35.110 --> 00:10:39.170
time reversal cannot
be a symmetry.

00:10:39.170 --> 00:10:41.350
So meaning, going backwards
and forwards in time

00:10:41.350 --> 00:10:43.600
is not symmetric.

00:10:43.600 --> 00:10:44.690
And you can test this.

00:10:44.690 --> 00:10:48.980
You can design experiments
which test [INAUDIBLE] the fact.

00:10:48.980 --> 00:10:52.840
You can also design experiments
which test CPT directly.

00:10:52.840 --> 00:10:55.090
But this is-- those are
all interesting questions,

00:10:55.090 --> 00:10:59.810
but we will not go into any
of those in this lecture.

00:10:59.810 --> 00:11:02.260
We will, however, come
back to understanding

00:11:02.260 --> 00:11:05.500
the origin of CP violation
in the standard model when

00:11:05.500 --> 00:11:11.470
we talk in more detail
about the weak interaction.

